#---
# purpose: "Clauses guarded on different tuple tags dispatch on the tag, not on the arity"
# oracle: oracle.exs
# oracle.stamp: de4548078359824a
#---
#
# fz-kdt.119. Two clauses matching `{:cont, n}` and `{:halt, n}` when `is_integer(n)`,
# reached with a value the compiler cannot fold away. The PLAN is right --
# one clause tests `{:cont, integer}` and the other `{:halt, integer}` -- but
# the runtime projection of those types used to be "a 2-tuple" for both, so
# the first clause took every value and this printed `1 1`, on `interp` and on
# `run` alike. Swap the clauses (`gat` below) and it printed `2 2`: which
# answer you got was which clause you wrote first.
#
# The tuple test now carries one sub-predicate per position per clause, and an
# atom position is one a value passes only by BEING that atom, so the tag is
# what routes the call. Both clause orders print the same two lines, which is
# the whole claim: dispatch on tagged tuples means what it says.
def tag({:cont, n}) when is_integer(n), do: 1
def tag({:halt, n}) when is_integer(n), do: 2

def gat({:halt, n}) when is_integer(n), do: 2
def gat({:cont, n}) when is_integer(n), do: 1

def pick(n) do
  if n > 0 do
    {:cont, n}
  else
    {:halt, n}
  end
end

def main() do
  dbg(tag(pick(1)))
  dbg(tag(pick(0)))
  dbg(gat(pick(1)))
  dbg(gat(pick(0)))
end
